IMPORT prelude
IMPORT `maireg-dates`

-- Motor Accident Injuries Regulation 2017 (NSW) -- the claims provisions (Parts 1-5 and 7), from the seat of the person
-- injured in a motor accident who claims statutory benefits or damages under the Motor Accident Injuries Act 2017.
--
-- SEAT: the claimant (the injured person, or a person claiming in connection with another's death or injury). Every
-- exported question is one that person would ask: "Is my injury a threshold injury?", "By when must I claim?", "Can I be
-- back-paid for the weeks before my late claim?", "Must I ask for an internal review first?", "Did the insurer have to
-- tell me my review rights?", "Is my claim exempt from claims assessment?", "By when must the insurer pay me?", "Is
-- interest owed to me?", "Can I be charged for a missed assessment?", "What is my car travel worth?". The insurer, the
-- Authority, the Commission and its President get no seat: what they may do is encoded as what it means for the claimant.
--
-- Provisions: cl 4 (threshold injury), cl 7 (minimum weekly amount), cl 8 (mental harm), cl 8A (late claims and back pay),
-- cl 9 (accommodation and travel damages), cl 10 and 11 (no internal review needed), cl 12 (review rights notice, a
-- regulative rule), cl 13 (further medical assessment), cl 14 (claims exempt from assessment), cl 15 (acceptance day),
-- cl 16 (payment of assessed damages and interest, a regulative rule), cl 17 (miscellaneous claims assessments), cl 18
-- (evidence restriction), cl 32 (missed medical assessment), cl 33 (car travel), cl 36 (service), cl 42 (savings, as a
-- gate). The parent Act's sections that these clauses work through (ss 1.6, 3.10, 6.13, 7.11, 7.24, 7.34, 7.38, 7.41,
-- 7.52, 11.5) are read from the deposited Act where a clause cannot be answered without them; each is named.
-- The costs clauses (Part 6, Schedules 1-3) are in maireg-costs.l4. Out (NOTHING for the claimant, or machinery): see
-- NOTES.md.
--
-- ANSWERS. A yes/no question answers JUST TRUE, JUST FALSE where the text's own words say no (a "means" definition, a
-- condition introduced by "if", "unless" or "where", a flat "may not", "does not apply", "not payable"), or NOTHING where
-- the text does not answer on the facts given; each NOTHING carries a "NOTHING-kind" comment.
--
-- Based on content from the New South Wales Legislation website (as collected by the Open Australian Legal Corpus on
-- 10 March 2025; Regulation version as at 22 September 2023, Act version as at 1 April 2023; unofficial, possibly out of
-- date). CC BY 4.0. BASELINE ENCODING. Not for public use as advice. See NOTES.md.

§ `Motor Accident Injuries Regulation 2017 -- claims`

§§ `The nouns`

-- cl 4 and s 1.6 of the Act. The injury the claimant asks about, as the clause names it.
DECLARE `The injury I am asking about` IS ONE OF
    `an injury to a spinal nerve root showing neurological signs other than radiculopathy`
    `an injury to a spinal nerve root showing radiculopathy`
    `acute stress disorder`
    `adjustment disorder`
    `another injury`

-- cl 10 and Schedule 1 cl 1(2)(g)-(i). The merit review matter the decision is about.
DECLARE `The decision I want reviewed is about` IS ONE OF
    `whether a request made of me under s 6.24 is reasonable, or whether I had a reasonable excuse for not complying`
    `whether I gave the insurer all relevant particulars of my claim under s 6.25`
    `whether the insurer may give me a direction under s 6.26`
    `whether my costs and expenses under s 8.10 are reasonable and necessary`
    `another merit review matter`

-- The two kinds of claim the Act knows.
DECLARE `The kind of claim` IS ONE OF
    `a claim for statutory benefits`
    `a claim for damages`

-- cl 12: what the clause means for the claimant.
DECLARE `What clause 12 means for me` IS ONE OF
    `the decision carries no right to internal or merit review, so clause 12 asks nothing of the insurer`
    `the insurer told me my review rights in writing with the decision, as clause 12 requires`
    `the insurer did not tell me my review rights in writing with the decision, as clause 12 requires`

-- cl 13: the ground for a further assessment.
DECLARE `The ground for referring my medical dispute again` IS ONE OF
    `my injury has deteriorated`
    `there is additional relevant information about my injury`
    `another ground`

-- cl 16: where the claimant's assessed damages stand.
DECLARE `Where my assessed damages stand` IS ONE OF
    `clause 16 does not apply, because the assessed damages are not payable under s 7.38(2)`
    `the insurer has not yet received all the advice on deductions, so the payment period has not started`
    `paid within the period, so no interest is payable`
    `the period for payment has not ended`
    `unpaid after the period for payment ended, so interest is payable`

-- cl 18 and s 7.52(4): the matter the evidence is about.
DECLARE `The matter the evidence is about` IS ONE OF
    `the degree of my permanent impairment`
    `whether my treatment and care is reasonable and necessary, or relates to my accident injury (s 3.24)`
    `whether treatment or care will improve my recovery (s 3.28)`
    `the degree of impairment of my earning capacity`
    `whether my injury is a threshold injury`
    `another matter`

-- cl 33: why the claimant travelled.
DECLARE `Why I travelled` IS ONE OF
    `to attend a medical assessment under Division 7.5`
    `to attend an examination or assessment the insurer arranged under s 6.27`
    `for something else`

-- The party and acts of the two regulative rules.
DECLARE `Insurer party` IS ONE OF `the insurer`
DECLARE `Insurer act` IS ONE OF
    `notify me in writing of my review rights`
    `pay me the assessed damages`

§§ `cl 4 -- threshold injury`

-- s 1.6(1) of the Act: "a threshold injury is, subject to this section, one or more of the following-- (a) a soft tissue
-- injury, (b) a psychological or psychiatric injury that is not a recognised psychiatric illness." s 1.6(2) excludes "an
-- injury to nerves" from soft tissue injury. s 1.6(4)(b): the regulations may include a specified injury.
-- cl 4(1) "An injury to a spinal nerve root that manifests in neurological signs (other than radiculopathy) is included as
-- a soft tissue injury for the purposes of the Act." cl 4(2) "Each of the following injuries is included as a threshold
-- injury ... (a) acute stress disorder, (b) adjustment disorder." cl 4(3): both as in DSM-5 (a borrowed term, asked).
-- Whether the injury is a threshold injury under s 1.6 of the Act alone is asked (the Act is a separate subject). The Act's
-- "is" is a definition, closed by the regulations' additions (fork F1); radiculopathy is "(other than X)" (fork F2).
@export Is my motor accident injury a threshold injury (the "minor injury" that limits benefits after 52 weeks and bars damages for non-economic loss), counting the injuries the Regulation adds (cl 4; Act s 1.6)?
GIVEN `my injury` IS A `The injury I am asking about` @desc The injury you are asking about. One of: "an injury to a spinal nerve root showing neurological signs other than radiculopathy", "an injury to a spinal nerve root showing radiculopathy", "acute stress disorder", "adjustment disorder" (both as defined in DSM-5), "another injury"
      `it is a threshold injury under the Act itself` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether, under section 1.6 of the Act alone, your injury is a soft tissue injury (to muscles, tendons, ligaments and the like, but not nerves or a rupture) or a psychological or psychiatric injury that is not a recognised psychiatric illness
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Is my injury a threshold injury` MEANS
    IF `it is a threshold injury under the Act itself`
    THEN JUST TRUE
    ELSE CONSIDER `my injury`
         WHEN `an injury to a spinal nerve root showing neurological signs other than radiculopathy` THEN JUST TRUE  -- cl 4(1)
         WHEN `acute stress disorder` THEN JUST TRUE  -- cl 4(2)(a)
         WHEN `adjustment disorder` THEN JUST TRUE  -- cl 4(2)(b)
         OTHERWISE JUST FALSE  -- s 1.6(1) "is ... one or more of the following", with nothing added (forks F1, F2)

§§ `cl 7 -- minimum weekly statutory benefits amount`

-- s 3.10(1): "the minimum weekly statutory benefits amount is the amount prescribed by the regulations." cl 7: "the
-- amount that is 2.5% of the maximum weekly statutory benefits amount." The maximum is $3,853 in s 3.9(1) of the Act, as
-- declared each year by the Authority under s 3.9(3) (not deposited; asked). No rounding is stated.
@export What is the minimum weekly statutory benefits amount for my weekly payments (cl 7; Act s 3.10)?
GIVEN `the maximum weekly statutory benefits amount` IS A NUMBER @desc The maximum weekly statutory benefits amount, in dollars, that applies on the date your weekly payment is payable ($3,853 in section 3.9(1) of the Act, as adjusted each year by the Authority's order)
GIVETH A NUMBER
`What is the minimum weekly statutory benefits amount` MEANS `the maximum weekly statutory benefits amount` TIMES 2.5%

§§ `cl 8 -- mental harm`

-- s 3.39 of the Act applies Part 3 (mental harm) of the Civil Liability Act 2002 to statutory benefits. cl 8(2): the
-- requirement in s 30(3) of that Act (to reduce damages for pure mental harm in the same proportion as the victim's
-- contributory negligence) "is to be read as a requirement to apply the at-fault statutory benefits limitations to the
-- payment of statutory benefits to the claimant in the same way as they apply to the payment of statutory benefits to
-- the victim." cl 8(3): those limitations are the Act's reductions or cessations for injured persons "wholly or mostly
-- at fault". Whether they apply to the victim is the Act's question, asked.
@export If I claim statutory benefits for pure mental harm because of another person's injury or death in the accident, are my weekly payments limited the same way as that person's (cl 8; Act s 3.39)?
GIVEN `I claim for pure mental harm because of the victim's injury or death` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether your claim is for pure mental harm (a psychological injury not following a physical injury of your own) in connection with another person (the victim) being injured or killed in the accident
      `the victim's weekly payments are limited because the victim was wholly or mostly at fault` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the victim's weekly statutory benefits are (or would be) reduced or stopped under Part 3 of the Act because the victim was wholly or mostly at fault in the accident
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Are my benefits limited by the victim's fault` MEANS
    IF (NOT `I claim for pure mental harm because of the victim's injury or death`)
    THEN NOTHING  -- NOTHING-kind (outside cl 8's scope; the clause is silent)
    ELSE IF `the victim's weekly payments are limited because the victim was wholly or mostly at fault`
         THEN JUST TRUE
         ELSE JUST FALSE  -- "in the same way as they apply ... to the victim": none apply to the victim

§§ `s 6.13 and cl 8A -- time for claiming statutory benefits, and back pay for a late claim`

-- s 6.13(2) of the Act: "If a claim for statutory benefits is not made within 28 days after the date of the motor
-- accident, weekly payments of statutory benefits are not payable in respect of any period before the claim is made,
-- unless permitted by the regulations." "Within 28 days after" ends on the 28th day after the accident (fork F3).
@export By what day must I claim statutory benefits so that weekly payments can be paid from the accident, not only from my claim (Act s 6.13(2))?
GIVEN `the day of my accident` IS A DATE @desc The date of the motor accident
GIVETH A DATE
`By what day must I claim to be paid weekly benefits from the accident` MEANS `the day of my accident` `plus days` 28

-- cl 8A(1)(a) "the claim is made within 3 months after the date of the motor accident" (fork F4).
@export If I miss the 28 days, by what day must I claim so that weekly payments for the time before my claim can still be paid, given a full and satisfactory explanation (cl 8A(1)(a))?
GIVEN `the day of my accident` IS A DATE @desc The date of the motor accident
GIVETH A DATE
`By what day must I claim to be back-paid for the weeks before a late claim` MEANS `the day of my accident` `plus months` 3

-- cl 8A(1)(b) a "full and satisfactory explanation for the delay". cl 8A(2) lists matters the decision-maker must
-- consider (awareness of the right, legal incapacity, illness or injury before the 28 days expired); the decision is the
-- insurer's (and on review the Commission's), so it is asked. cl 8A(3) "An explanation ... is taken to be a full and
-- satisfactory explanation ... if the insurer has not rejected the explanation within 14 days after receiving the
-- explanation." A rejection on the 14th day after receipt is in time (fork F3).
@export Is my explanation for claiming late taken to be full and satisfactory (cl 8A(1)(b), (2), (3))?
GIVEN `I gave the insurer an explanation for the delay` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you gave the insurer an explanation for not claiming within 28 days after the accident
      `the day the insurer received my explanation` IS A DATE @desc The day the insurer received your explanation (any date if you gave none)
      `the insurer rejected my explanation` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the insurer has rejected your explanation
      `the day the insurer rejected it` IS A DATE @desc The day the insurer rejected your explanation (any date if it has not)
      `my explanation is full and satisfactory` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether your explanation is in fact full and satisfactory, as the insurer (or, on review, the Commission) decides it, considering whether before the 28 days ran out you knew you could claim, were under a legal incapacity, or were prevented by illness or injury
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Is my explanation for the late claim full and satisfactory` MEANS
    BRANCH IF (NOT `I gave the insurer an explanation for the delay`) THEN JUST FALSE  -- cl 8A(1)(b) unmet
           IF (NOT `rejected in time`) THEN JUST TRUE  -- cl 8A(3), "is taken to be"
           OTHERWISE JUST `my explanation is full and satisfactory`  -- the decision-maker's judgment, asked
    WHERE
        `rejected in time` MEANS
                `the insurer rejected my explanation`
            AND `the day the insurer rejected it` `is on or before` (`the day the insurer received my explanation` `plus days` 14)

-- s 6.13(2) with cl 8A(1), (4) and cl 42. cl 8A(4) "This clause does not apply to a motor accident occurring before
-- 1 April 2023." cl 42 keeps the Act's former Schedule 2 cl 2(c) for those accidents; its text is not deposited, so
-- they answer NOTHING.
@export Can weekly statutory benefits be paid to me for the time before I made my claim (Act s 6.13(2); cl 8A; cl 42)?
GIVEN `the day of my accident` IS A DATE @desc The date of the motor accident
      `the day I made my claim` IS A DATE @desc The day you made your claim for statutory benefits
      `I gave the insurer an explanation for the delay` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you gave the insurer an explanation for not claiming within 28 days after the accident
      `the day the insurer received my explanation` IS A DATE @desc The day the insurer received your explanation (any date if you gave none)
      `the insurer rejected my explanation` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the insurer has rejected your explanation
      `the day the insurer rejected it` IS A DATE @desc The day the insurer rejected your explanation (any date if it has not)
      `my explanation is full and satisfactory` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether your explanation is in fact full and satisfactory, as the insurer (or, on review, the Commission) decides it
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Can I be paid weekly benefits for the time before my claim` MEANS
    BRANCH IF `the day I made my claim` `is on or before` (`By what day must I claim to be paid weekly benefits from the accident` `the day of my accident`)
           THEN JUST TRUE  -- s 6.13(2) does not bite
           IF `the day of my accident` `is before` (`the date` 1 4 2023)
           THEN NOTHING  -- NOTHING-kind (cl 8A(4), cl 42: the former Schedule 2 cl 2(c) of the Act governs; not deposited)
           IF (NOT (`the day I made my claim` `is on or before` (`By what day must I claim to be back-paid for the weeks before a late claim` `the day of my accident`)))
           THEN JUST FALSE  -- cl 8A(1)(a) unmet; s 6.13(2) "not payable"
           OTHERWISE `Is my explanation for the late claim full and satisfactory` `I gave the insurer an explanation for the delay` `the day the insurer received my explanation` `the insurer rejected my explanation` `the day the insurer rejected it` `my explanation is full and satisfactory`

§§ `cl 9 -- accommodation and travel damages`

-- cl 9 "The kind of accommodation or travel for which damages may be awarded (subject to Division 4.2 of the Act) is any
-- accommodation or travel for which the claimant has incurred, or is likely to incur, a cost as a result of the injury
-- caused by the motor accident." Read as a definition of the kind (fork F5). Division 4.2's limits are the Act's.
@export Can damages be awarded to me for this accommodation or travel, subject to the Act's limits on damages (cl 9; Act s 4.5)?
GIVEN `I have incurred or am likely to incur the cost because of my accident injury` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you have paid, or are likely to have to pay, for this accommodation or travel as a result of the injury the motor accident caused
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Can I claim damages for this accommodation or travel` MEANS
    IF `I have incurred or am likely to incur the cost because of my accident injury` THEN JUST TRUE ELSE JUST FALSE

§§ `cl 10, 11 -- when no internal review is needed`

-- s 7.11(1) "A merit review application may not be made in relation to a reviewable decision until the decision has been
-- the subject of an internal review by the insurer". s 7.11(2): not if the insurer failed to complete the internal review
-- and notify the decision in time, or declined to conduct one. s 7.11(3): "This section does not apply to a reviewable
-- decision about a merit review matter of a kind prescribed by the regulations." cl 10 prescribes four. Whether the
-- decision is a reviewable decision at all is the Act's (Schedule 2), assumed (fork F6).
@export Must I ask the insurer for an internal review before I apply for a merit review of its decision (cl 10; Act s 7.11)?
GIVEN `the decision is about` IS A `The decision I want reviewed is about` @desc What the insurer's decision is about. One of: "whether a request made of me under s 6.24 is reasonable, or whether I had a reasonable excuse for not complying", "whether I gave the insurer all relevant particulars of my claim under s 6.25", "whether the insurer may give me a direction under s 6.26", "whether my costs and expenses under s 8.10 are reasonable and necessary", "another merit review matter"
      `the insurer failed to complete an internal review in time or declined to do one` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you already asked for an internal review and the insurer failed to complete it and tell you the result as and when required, or declined to conduct it
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Must I ask for an internal review before a merit review` MEANS
    IF `the insurer failed to complete an internal review in time or declined to do one`
    THEN JUST FALSE  -- s 7.11(2)
    ELSE CONSIDER `the decision is about`
         WHEN `another merit review matter` THEN JUST TRUE  -- s 7.11(1), "may not ... until"
         OTHERWISE JUST FALSE  -- cl 10 with s 7.11(3)

-- s 7.41(1): a claimant may not refer a dispute about an insurer's decision for miscellaneous claims assessment until it
-- has been internally reviewed; (2) not if the Motor Accident Guidelines say so, or the insurer failed or declined;
-- (3) not for a matter the regulations prescribe. cl 11 prescribes a dispute "about which insurer is the insurer of the
-- at-fault motor vehicle for the purposes of section 3.3".
@export Must I ask the insurer for an internal review before I refer a dispute about its decision for a miscellaneous claims assessment (cl 11; Act s 7.41)?
GIVEN `the dispute is about which insurer insures the at-fault vehicle` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the dispute is about which insurer is the insurer of the at-fault motor vehicle (section 3.3 of the Act)
      `the Motor Accident Guidelines say no internal review is needed for this matter` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Motor Accident Guidelines say that no internal review is required for decisions about this kind of matter
      `the insurer failed to complete an internal review in time or declined to do one` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the insurer failed to complete an internal review and tell you the result as and when required, or declined to conduct one
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Must I ask for an internal review before a miscellaneous claims assessment` MEANS
    IF     `the dispute is about which insurer insures the at-fault vehicle`
        OR `the Motor Accident Guidelines say no internal review is needed for this matter`
        OR `the insurer failed to complete an internal review in time or declined to do one`
    THEN JUST FALSE  -- cl 11 with s 7.41(3); s 7.41(2)
    ELSE JUST TRUE  -- s 7.41(1), "may not ... until"

§§ `cl 12 -- the insurer must tell me my review rights`

-- cl 12(1) "The insurer must notify a claimant in writing of any right of the claimant to request an internal review or
-- apply for a merit review in relation to a decision of the insurer. Maximum penalty--5 penalty units." (2) "The
-- notification must be given when the insurer notifies the claimant of the decision". (3) It extends to decisions that
-- can go to merit review only after internal review. Time 0 is the day the insurer notifies the decision; "when" is
-- WITHIN 0. The penalty unit's dollar value is set by another Act (not deposited).
GIVETH A DEONTIC `Insurer party` `Insurer act`
`cl 12 -- the insurer must tell me my review rights in writing with its decision` MEANS
    PARTY `the insurer`
    MUST `notify me in writing of my review rights`
    WITHIN 0
    HENCE FULFILLED
    LEST BREACH BY `the insurer` BECAUSE "the insurer did not notify the claimant in writing of the right to internal or merit review when it notified the decision (cl 12; maximum penalty 5 penalty units)"

@export When the insurer told me its decision, did it have to tell me in writing about my right to an internal review or merit review, and did it (cl 12)?
GIVEN `the decision carries a right to internal review or merit review` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you have a right to ask for an internal review, or to apply for a merit review, of this decision of the insurer (including a right that arises only after an internal review)
      `the insurer told me of those rights in writing` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the insurer has told you in writing about your right to an internal review or a merit review of this decision
      `it told me at the same time as the decision` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether that written notice came when the insurer told you the decision (with the decision), rather than later
GIVETH A `What clause 12 means for me`
`Did the insurer tell me my review rights` MEANS
    IF (NOT `the decision carries a right to internal review or merit review`)
    THEN `the decision carries no right to internal or merit review, so clause 12 asks nothing of the insurer`
    ELSE CONSIDER EVALTRACE `cl 12 -- the insurer must tell me my review rights in writing with its decision` 0 `the events`
         WHEN FULFILLED THEN `the insurer told me my review rights in writing with the decision, as clause 12 requires`
         OTHERWISE `the insurer did not tell me my review rights in writing with the decision, as clause 12 requires`
    WHERE
        `the events` MEANS
            IF `the insurer told me of those rights in writing` AND `it told me at the same time as the decision`
            THEN LIST (EVENT `the insurer` `notify me in writing of my review rights` 0)
            ELSE LIST (WAITUNTIL 1)

§§ `cl 13 -- further medical assessment`

-- s 7.24(2): a claimant may refer a medical dispute again "but only on the grounds prescribed by the regulations".
-- s 7.24(3): "may not be referred again ... on more than one occasion". cl 13(1): the grounds are "deterioration of the
-- injury or additional relevant information about the injury"; (2) "may not be referred again ... unless the
-- deterioration or additional information is such as to be capable of having a material effect on the outcome of the
-- previous assessment" (a judgment, asked). The President's satisfaction (s 7.24(5)) is the gate this answers.
@export Can I refer my medical dispute for assessment again (cl 13; Act s 7.24(2), (3))?
GIVEN `my ground` IS A `The ground for referring my medical dispute again` @desc Your ground for asking for a further medical assessment. One of: "my injury has deteriorated", "there is additional relevant information about my injury", "another ground"
      `it could materially change the outcome of the previous assessment` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the deterioration or the additional information is capable of having a material effect on the outcome of the previous medical assessment
      `a party has already referred this dispute again once` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you or the insurer has already referred this medical dispute for a further assessment once before
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Can I have my medical dispute assessed again` MEANS
    BRANCH IF `a party has already referred this dispute again once` THEN JUST FALSE  -- s 7.24(3)
           IF `my ground` EQUALS `another ground` THEN JUST FALSE  -- s 7.24(2), "only on the grounds prescribed"
           IF `it could materially change the outcome of the previous assessment` THEN JUST TRUE
           OTHERWISE JUST FALSE  -- cl 13(2), "may not ... unless"

§§ `cl 14 -- claims exempt from claims assessment`

-- s 7.34(1) "A claim is exempt from assessment under this Division if-- (a) the claim is of a kind specified in the
-- regulations ..., or (b) the Commission has made a preliminary assessment of the claim and has determined (with the
-- approval of the President) that the claim is not suitable for assessment". cl 14 specifies (a)-(e). An exempt claim
-- gets a certificate letting the claimant go to court (s 7.34(2)); Schedule 1's maximum costs then do not apply (cl 24).
@export Is my claim for damages exempt from claims assessment, so that I can go to court without one (cl 14; Act s 7.34)?
GIVEN `I am a person under legal incapacity` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you are a person under legal incapacity (section 7.47 of the Act: for example a child under 18, or an adult who cannot manage their affairs)
      `it is a Compensation to Relatives Act action for a person under legal incapacity` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the claim involves an action under the Compensation to Relatives Act 1897 brought on behalf of a person under legal incapacity
      `the claim is against someone other than an insurer` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the claim is made against a person other than an insurer
      `the insurer has alleged fraud against me in writing` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the insurer has told you in writing that it alleges you engaged in conduct contravening section 6.41 (fraud on the scheme)
      `the insurer has declined to indemnify the owner or driver in writing` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the insurer has, by written notice to you and to the vehicle's owner or driver, declined to indemnify the owner or driver under the third-party policy
      `the Commission found the claim not suitable for assessment` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Commission made a preliminary assessment and determined, with the President's approval, that the claim is not suitable for assessment
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Is my claim exempt from claims assessment` MEANS
    IF     `I am a person under legal incapacity`
        OR `it is a Compensation to Relatives Act action for a person under legal incapacity`
        OR `the claim is against someone other than an insurer`
        OR `the insurer has alleged fraud against me in writing`
        OR `the insurer has declined to indemnify the owner or driver in writing`
        OR `the Commission found the claim not suitable for assessment`
    THEN JUST TRUE
    ELSE JUST FALSE  -- s 7.34(1) "is exempt ... if" (fork F7)

§§ `cl 15, 16 -- acceptance and payment of assessed damages`

-- cl 15(3) "the assessment acceptance day is the earlier of ... (a) the day on which the insurer receives a notice under
-- this clause of the claimant's acceptance of an amount of damages, (b) the day that is 21 days after the certificate of
-- assessment is issued to the insurer."
@export What is the "assessment acceptance day" for my assessed damages, from which the insurer's time to pay runs (cl 15(3))?
GIVEN `the day the certificate of assessment was issued to the insurer` IS A DATE @desc The day the certificate of assessment of your damages was issued to the insurer
      `I sent the insurer written notice that I accept the assessed damages` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you gave the insurer written notice that you accept the amount of damages assessed
      `the day the insurer received my notice of acceptance` IS A DATE @desc The day the insurer received your notice of acceptance (any date if you sent none)
GIVETH A DATE
`What is my assessment acceptance day` MEANS
    IF `I sent the insurer written notice that I accept the assessed damages`
    THEN `the earlier of` `the day the insurer received my notice of acceptance` `and` `the 21st day`
    ELSE `the 21st day`
    WHERE
        `the 21st day` MEANS `the day the certificate of assessment was issued to the insurer` `plus days` 21

-- s 7.38(2): the assessed amount is binding on the insurer, and it "must pay", if "(a) the insurer admits that liability
-- under the claim, and (b) the claimant accepts that amount of damages in settlement of the claim within 21 days after
-- the certificate of assessment is issued." Acceptance is read as the insurer's receipt of the claimant's notice (fork F8).
GIVEN `the day the certificate of assessment was issued to the insurer` IS A DATE
      `I sent the insurer written notice that I accept the assessed damages` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the day the insurer received my notice of acceptance` IS A DATE
      `the insurer admits liability` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the assessed damages are payable under s 7.38(2)` IF
        `the insurer admits liability`
    AND `I sent the insurer written notice that I accept the assessed damages`
    AND `the day the insurer received my notice of acceptance` `is on or before` (`the day the certificate of assessment was issued to the insurer` `plus days` 21)

-- cl 16(1) "must be paid within 28 days after the assessment acceptance day, except as otherwise provided"; (2)-(3) the
-- insurer notifies any deduction it must make by law to the person it is payable to, within 14 days after the acceptance
-- day, asking for advice of the amount; (4) "The insurer must pay the balance ... within 28 days after all advice
-- requested under this clause in relation to deductions for a claim has been received by the insurer."
@export By what day must the insurer pay me my assessed damages (cl 15, 16(1), (4); Act s 7.38)?
GIVEN `the day the certificate of assessment was issued to the insurer` IS A DATE @desc The day the certificate of assessment of your damages was issued to the insurer
      `I sent the insurer written notice that I accept the assessed damages` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you gave the insurer written notice that you accept the amount of damages assessed
      `the day the insurer received my notice of acceptance` IS A DATE @desc The day the insurer received your notice of acceptance (any date if you sent none)
      `the insurer admits liability` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the insurer admits liability under your claim
      `the insurer must make deductions by law` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the insurer is required by law to deduct amounts from your damages and pay them to someone else (for example a Medicare or Centrelink repayment)
      `the insurer has received all the advice on deductions` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the insurer has received all the advice it asked for about the amounts of those deductions
      `the day the insurer received the last advice` IS A DATE @desc The day the insurer received the last of that advice (any date if not yet, or if there are no deductions)
GIVETH A MAYBE DATE
`By what day must the insurer pay my assessed damages` MEANS
    BRANCH IF (NOT (`the assessed damages are payable under s 7.38(2)` `the day the certificate of assessment was issued to the insurer` `I sent the insurer written notice that I accept the assessed damages` `the day the insurer received my notice of acceptance` `the insurer admits liability`))
           THEN NOTHING  -- NOTHING-kind (cl 16 does not reach damages that are not payable; the text is silent)
           IF (NOT `the insurer must make deductions by law`)
           THEN JUST ((`What is my assessment acceptance day` `the day the certificate of assessment was issued to the insurer` `I sent the insurer written notice that I accept the assessed damages` `the day the insurer received my notice of acceptance`) `plus days` 28)  -- cl 16(1)
           IF `the insurer has received all the advice on deductions`
           THEN JUST (`the day the insurer received the last advice` `plus days` 28)  -- cl 16(4), the balance
           OTHERWISE NOTHING  -- NOTHING-kind (cl 16(4): the period has not begun; no date can be given yet)

-- The duty to pay, as a rule. Time 0 is the day the period starts (the acceptance day, or the day the last advice on
-- deductions arrived); `the days to pay` is 28.
GIVEN `the days to pay` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A DEONTIC `Insurer party` `Insurer act`
`cl 16 -- the insurer must pay the assessed damages within the period` `the days to pay` MEANS
    PARTY `the insurer`
    MUST `pay me the assessed damages`
    WITHIN `the days to pay`
    HENCE FULFILLED
    LEST BREACH BY `the insurer` BECAUSE "assessed damages unpaid after the end of the period for payment; interest is payable on the unpaid amount at 75% of the Civil Procedure Act 2005 s 101 rate (cl 16(5))"

-- cl 16(5) "Interest is payable by the insurer on so much of the assessed amount of damages as remains unpaid after the
-- end of the relevant period for payment". Payment is counted on the day it is made; a payment before the period starts
-- counts at its start.
@export Has the insurer paid my assessed damages in time, or is interest now payable to me on them (cl 16; Act s 7.38(3))?
GIVEN `the day the certificate of assessment was issued to the insurer` IS A DATE @desc The day the certificate of assessment of your damages was issued to the insurer
      `I sent the insurer written notice that I accept the assessed damages` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you gave the insurer written notice that you accept the amount of damages assessed
      `the day the insurer received my notice of acceptance` IS A DATE @desc The day the insurer received your notice of acceptance (any date if you sent none)
      `the insurer admits liability` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the insurer admits liability under your claim
      `the insurer must make deductions by law` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the insurer is required by law to deduct amounts from your damages and pay them to someone else
      `the insurer has received all the advice on deductions` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the insurer has received all the advice it asked for about the amounts of those deductions
      `the day the insurer received the last advice` IS A DATE @desc The day the insurer received the last of that advice (any date if not yet, or if there are no deductions)
      `I have been paid in full` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the insurer has paid you the whole of the assessed damages (or the balance after deductions)
      `the day I was paid` IS A DATE @desc The day you were paid in full (any date if not yet)
      `today` IS A DATE @desc Today's date
GIVETH A `Where my assessed damages stand`
`Is interest payable on my assessed damages` MEANS
    CONSIDER `By what day must the insurer pay my assessed damages` `the day the certificate of assessment was issued to the insurer` `I sent the insurer written notice that I accept the assessed damages` `the day the insurer received my notice of acceptance` `the insurer admits liability` `the insurer must make deductions by law` `the insurer has received all the advice on deductions` `the day the insurer received the last advice`
    WHEN NOTHING THEN
        IF `payable`
        THEN `the insurer has not yet received all the advice on deductions, so the payment period has not started`
        ELSE `clause 16 does not apply, because the assessed damages are not payable under s 7.38(2)`
    WHEN JUST `the last day` THEN
        IF (NOT `I have been paid in full`) AND `today` `is on or before` `the last day`
        THEN `the period for payment has not ended`
        ELSE CONSIDER EVALTRACE (`cl 16 -- the insurer must pay the assessed damages within the period` 28) 0 (`the events` (`the last day` `plus days` (0 MINUS 28)))
             WHEN FULFILLED THEN `paid within the period, so no interest is payable`
             OTHERWISE `unpaid after the period for payment ended, so interest is payable`
    WHERE
        `payable` MEANS `the assessed damages are payable under s 7.38(2)` `the day the certificate of assessment was issued to the insurer` `I sent the insurer written notice that I accept the assessed damages` `the day the insurer received my notice of acceptance` `the insurer admits liability`
        `the events` `the start` MEANS
            IF `I have been paid in full`
            THEN LIST (EVENT `the insurer` `pay me the assessed damages` (`the larger of` 0 `and` (`the days from` `the start` `to` `the day I was paid`)))
            ELSE LIST (WAITUNTIL (`the days from` `the start` `to` `today`))

-- cl 16(5) "The rate of any such interest is 75% of the rate prescribed for the purposes of section 101 of the Civil
-- Procedure Act 2005 for the period concerned." The s 101 rate is set under that Act (not deposited); asked.
@export At what yearly rate is interest payable to me on assessed damages the insurer pays late (cl 16(5))?
GIVEN `the Civil Procedure Act section 101 rate` IS A NUMBER @desc The interest rate prescribed for section 101 of the Civil Procedure Act 2005 (interest on judgment debts) for the period concerned, as a percentage per year (for example 10.25 for 10.25%)
GIVETH A NUMBER
`At what rate is interest payable on my late assessed damages` MEANS `the Civil Procedure Act section 101 rate` TIMES 75%

§§ `cl 17 -- miscellaneous claims assessments`

-- cl 17(c) reads s 7.38(1) as a reference to "(i) an assessment of a dispute about a miscellaneous claims assessment
-- matter relating to a claim for statutory benefits being binding, subject to section 3.44 ..., and (ii) an assessment of
-- a dispute about a miscellaneous claims assessment matter relating to a claim for damages not being binding."
@export Is the miscellaneous claims assessment of my dispute with the insurer binding (cl 17(c); Act s 7.38(1))?
GIVEN `my claim` IS A `The kind of claim` @desc The claim the dispute relates to. One of: "a claim for statutory benefits", "a claim for damages"
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Is the miscellaneous claims assessment of my dispute binding` MEANS
    CONSIDER `my claim`
    WHEN `a claim for statutory benefits` THEN JUST TRUE  -- subject to s 3.44 (fault findings do not bind a common law claim)
    WHEN `a claim for damages` THEN JUST FALSE

§§ `cl 18 -- evidence restriction`

-- s 7.52(1): in court proceedings for damages, a merit review, a medical assessment or a claims assessment, "evidence
-- given by a health practitioner in relation to a medical matter concerning an injured person is not admissible unless
-- (a) the practitioner is a treating health practitioner of the injured person, or (b) the practitioner is authorised by
-- the Motor Accident Guidelines". s 7.52(4) "medical matter means (a) the degree of permanent impairment ..., or (b) any
-- medical assessment matter of a kind prescribed by the regulations". cl 18 prescribes (a)-(d). "means" closes the list.
@export Does section 7.52 of the Act stop a health practitioner's evidence about this matter being admitted in my case (cl 18; Act s 7.52)?
GIVEN `the evidence is about` IS A `The matter the evidence is about` @desc What the health practitioner's evidence is about. One of: "the degree of my permanent impairment", "whether my treatment and care is reasonable and necessary, or relates to my accident injury (s 3.24)", "whether treatment or care will improve my recovery (s 3.28)", "the degree of impairment of my earning capacity", "whether my injury is a threshold injury", "another matter"
      `it is for court proceedings for damages or a merit review, medical assessment or claims assessment` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the evidence is to be given in court proceedings for damages, or in a merit review, medical assessment or claims assessment under Part 7 of the Act
      `the practitioner is one of my treating health practitioners` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the health practitioner treats (or treated) you
      `the Motor Accident Guidelines authorise the practitioner` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the practitioner is authorised by the Motor Accident Guidelines to give evidence in the proceedings (for example as a jointly appointed or Authority-appointed expert)
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Is this health practitioner's evidence barred` MEANS
    BRANCH IF (NOT `it is for court proceedings for damages or a merit review, medical assessment or claims assessment`)
           THEN NOTHING  -- NOTHING-kind (a scope limit with no "only": the section is silent elsewhere)
           IF `the evidence is about` EQUALS `another matter` THEN JUST FALSE  -- not a "medical matter" ("means")
           IF `the practitioner is one of my treating health practitioners` OR `the Motor Accident Guidelines authorise the practitioner`
           THEN JUST FALSE  -- the "unless" is met (fork F9)
           OTHERWISE JUST TRUE

§§ `cl 32, 33 -- assessments: missed appointments and travel`

-- cl 32(1) "The Authority may recover from a claimant all or part of the costs reasonably incurred by the President as a
-- consequence of the claimant-- (a) failing to attend an appointment scheduled by the President for a medical
-- assessment ..., or (b) cancelling such an appointment within 72 hours of the scheduled time." (2) "The Authority may not
-- recover the costs if the claimant has a reasonable excuse" (a judgment, asked). A cancellation exactly 72 hours ahead
-- is "within 72 hours" (fork F10). Hours are whole hours before the scheduled time.
@export Can the Authority make me pay the costs of a medical assessment appointment I missed or cancelled (cl 32)?
GIVEN `I failed to attend the appointment` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you did not attend an appointment the President scheduled for a medical assessment of you under Division 7.5 of the Act
      `I cancelled the appointment` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you cancelled the appointment
      `how many hours before the appointment I cancelled` IS A NUMBER @desc If you cancelled, how many hours before the scheduled time you cancelled (0 if you did not cancel)
      `I had a reasonable excuse` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you had a reasonable excuse for not attending or for cancelling (for example a sudden illness or emergency)
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Can the Authority recover the costs of my missed assessment` MEANS
    BRANCH IF (NOT (`I failed to attend the appointment` OR (`I cancelled the appointment` AND `how many hours before the appointment I cancelled` AT MOST 72)))
           THEN NOTHING  -- NOTHING-kind (a grant limited by its own words; the case falls outside it)
           IF `I had a reasonable excuse` THEN JUST FALSE  -- cl 32(2), "may not"
           OTHERWISE JUST TRUE  -- all or part of the costs reasonably incurred

-- cl 33 "the cost of travel by a private motor vehicle for the purposes of either of the following is to be calculated at
-- the rate of $0.66 per kilometre-- (a) attending a medical assessment under Division 7.5 of the Act, (b) attending a
-- medical or other health related examination, a rehabilitation assessment, an assessment to determine functional and
-- vocational capacity or any other assessment under section 6.27". No rounding is stated.
@export How much is my travel by private car to a medical assessment or insurer-arranged examination worth (cl 33; Act ss 6.27(5), 7.28(5))?
GIVEN `why I travelled` IS A `Why I travelled` @desc Why you travelled. One of: "to attend a medical assessment under Division 7.5", "to attend an examination or assessment the insurer arranged under s 6.27" (a medical or health examination, rehabilitation assessment, or functional and vocational capacity assessment), "for something else"
      `the kilometres I drove` IS A NUMBER @desc The distance you travelled by private motor vehicle for the purpose, in kilometres
GIVETH A MAYBE NUMBER
`How much is my car travel to an assessment worth` MEANS
    CONSIDER `why I travelled`
    WHEN `for something else` THEN NOTHING  -- NOTHING-kind (outside cl 33; the text is silent)
    OTHERWISE JUST (`the kilometres I drove` TIMES 0.66)

§§ `cl 36 -- service on my lawyer or representative`

-- s 11.5(2): a document is sufficiently served on a person in connection with a claim for damages if served on an
-- Australian legal practitioner acting for the person in that claim. cl 36(2) adds the same for a claim for statutory
-- benefits; cl 36(3): on a person under legal incapacity, if served on the person's appointed legal representative.
-- Other methods (s 11.5(1), cl 36(1)) need facts not asked here: NOTHING.
@export Is a document about my claim served on me if it is served on my lawyer, or on my appointed legal representative (cl 36(2), (3); Act s 11.5(2))?
GIVEN `it was served on a lawyer acting for me in the claim` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the document was served on an Australian legal practitioner acting for you in connection with your claim (for statutory benefits or for damages)
      `I am a person under legal incapacity` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you are a person under legal incapacity (section 7.47 of the Act)
      `it was served on my appointed legal representative` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the document was served on your appointed legal representative
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Is a document served on my lawyer served on me` MEANS
    IF     `it was served on a lawyer acting for me in the claim`
        OR (`I am a person under legal incapacity` AND `it was served on my appointed legal representative`)
    THEN JUST TRUE
    ELSE NOTHING  -- NOTHING-kind (other methods of service, s 11.5(1) and cl 36(1), turn on facts not asked)
